A||||4||sluppy123||General Question about a turbo-charged engine||||1||
Z||000000||sluppy123||08-12-2001||10:51 PM||||What Happens with the EGR when the intake goes under boost. I know that the EGR in closed at idle and WOT, however a Turbo'ed engine Can and does make boost throughout the throttle range. So this means I'm losing boost through the EGR when I'm accelerating hard but not at WOT. <P>Am I wrong? Anyone know how a Factory installed turbo engine handles this problem, if anything is done.<P>------------------<BR>-Brian, Toolmaker<BR>Lincoln, NE<BR>87' GT-Galaxy silver mettalic, Turbo Charged, Modified 2.8, Eibach Sport springs, RSM side scoops, Mr Mikes leather, 17" Mille Miglia's. <BR>              <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1711832&a=12781563&p=48213929&Sequence=0"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Primaris||08-12-2001||11:55 PM||||Ok these are my thought but I don't have practical experience.  When the EGR opens the force of the exhaust will be greater than the force of the boost.  The boost therefore will not travel down the exhaust.  Think of it in terms of water pipes.  If the water is spinning an impeller that is pushing water through the engine.  The source (exhaust) water is more forceful than the boost water. So it shouldn't make it through the EGR stream.  Anyway just my thoughts.<P>------------------<BR>Signatures are a waste of bandwidth!||||reg||1
Z||000002||sluppy123||08-13-2001||04:47 PM||||Anyone know how much pressure is in the exhaust? I think I read somewhere that is was 3-5 psi at WOT on the stock V-6 dual exhaust. Does this sound about right?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Neal||08-13-2001||08:56 PM||||The EGR opens as a combination of ECM controll and vaccume lines when your car is under boost there will be positive pressure int he vaccume lines, preventing the EGR from opening.<P>Im not 100% positive on this but it seems to make sence.<P>Also the EGR opening is dime sized, where as the rest of the ports are very large.  my guess is that the EGR is less than 3% of the total openings in the intake.  I am guessing but my upper plenum is in front of me.  So at most you would loose 3% of your total boost.||||reg||1
Z||000004||terryk||08-13-2001||10:27 PM||||On my V6, I measured 5 PSI backpressure at the O2 sensor port. This is under load and at 6000 RPM. Not terrible. When I installed the SC with the stock exhaust, I hit 10+. Then new system is 3-4 PSI at full boost at 5500.<P>It think it's unlikely the exhaust pressure will open the EGR. And yes, if you allow boost in the vacuum line, it will tend to try and force it closed from the top. <P>If the EGR opens, it will hit the boost in the intake. Higher pressure to low pressure, so I would expect air to flow into the exhaust if it opens at all. But if the boost increases the exhaust back-pressure.....<P>A little detail with blow-thru systems. No vacuum under even minor boost. The SC engines have a port before and after the rotors to give vacuum even under mild boost. Of course at WOT there's not vacuum (or very little).<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 08-13-2001).]||||reg||1
